From: ChromiumOS-NVIDIA-cross <dev@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] minigbm: add an nvidia-drm dumb-buffer backend

ChromeOS allocates graphics buffers through minigbm rather than Mesa's GBM, and
minigbm matches backends by DRM driver name. It already ships backends for
nouveau, radeon, evdi and others, but nothing matching "nvidia-drm", so on a
system whose display is driven by NVIDIA's driver minigbm finds no backend and
Chrome has nothing to render into.

NVIDIA's nvidia-drm implements the standard DRM dumb-buffer ioctls when loaded
with modeset=1, so the existing generic dumb backend is sufficient. This adds
one registration using INIT_DUMB_DRIVER_WITH_NAME, needed rather than
INIT_DUMB_DRIVER because "nvidia-drm" contains a hyphen and so cannot be a C
identifier -- the same reason sun4i_drm already uses that form.

Scope and limitations:

  - Dumb buffers are linear and allocated by the kernel. This is enough to get
    the ChromeOS UI on screen and is deliberately the smallest possible change.
  - It does not provide GPU-optimal tiled allocation. Accelerated scanout, where
    Chrome renders into buffers the display engine reads directly, requires
    NVIDIA's libnvidia-allocator and is a separate piece of work.
  - Requires nvidia-drm loaded with modeset=1. With modeset=0 (the Stage 2
    default) no DRM device is registered and this backend is simply never matched,
    which is harmless.

Tested against minigbm main as of the commit this patch was generated from.

--- a/drv.c	2026-09-26 08:00:09.835012896 -0500
+++ b/drv.c	2026-09-26 08:00:09.843367029 -0500
@@ -54,6 +54,7 @@
 extern const struct backend backend_mediatek;
 extern const struct backend backend_meson;
 extern const struct backend backend_nouveau;
+extern const struct backend backend_nvidia_drm;
 extern const struct backend backend_komeda;
 extern const struct backend backend_radeon;
 extern const struct backend backend_rockchip;
@@ -84,7 +85,10 @@
 	&backend_evdi,	    &backend_komeda,	&backend_marvell, &backend_mediatek,
 	&backend_meson,	    &backend_nouveau,	&backend_radeon,  &backend_rockchip,
 	&backend_sun4i_drm, &backend_synaptics, &backend_udl,	  &backend_virtgpu,
-	&backend_vkms,	    &backend_mock
+	&backend_vkms,
+	/* NVIDIA discrete GPU via nvidia-drm (requires modeset=1). */
+	&backend_nvidia_drm,
+	&backend_mock
 };
 
 void drv_preload(bool load)
--- a/dumb_driver.c	2026-09-26 08:00:09.835043433 -0500
+++ b/dumb_driver.c	2026-09-26 08:00:09.843288223 -0500
@@ -70,6 +70,16 @@
 INIT_DUMB_DRIVER(marvell)
 INIT_DUMB_DRIVER(meson)
 INIT_DUMB_DRIVER(nouveau)
+/*
+ * NVIDIA's nvidia-drm implements the DRM dumb-buffer ioctls when loaded with
+ * modeset=1, so the generic dumb backend is sufficient to get the ChromeOS UI on
+ * screen. The DRM driver name contains a hyphen, which is not valid in a C
+ * identifier, hence the _WITH_NAME form (same as sun4i_drm below).
+ *
+ * Dumb buffers are linear and unaccelerated. This is enough for Chrome to present,
+ * but GPU-optimal scanout needs libnvidia-allocator instead.
+ */
+INIT_DUMB_DRIVER_WITH_NAME(nvidia_drm, "nvidia-drm")
 INIT_DUMB_DRIVER(radeon)
 INIT_DUMB_DRIVER_WITH_NAME(sun4i_drm, "sun4i-drm")
 INIT_DUMB_DRIVER(synaptics)
